                  <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base mb-1">1. Independence Palace (Reunification Palace)</h3>
                  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                    The former presidential palace preserved as it stood on April 30, 1975. It provides the political contrast to Cu Chi's guerrilla tactics. Visitors explore presidential staterooms, the telecommunications war command bunker in the basement, and vintage military hardware on the grounds. Requires approximately 75–90 minutes.
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs">
                  <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base mb-1">2. War Remnants Museum</h3>
                  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                    One of Vietnam's most visited museums, housing heavy military aircraft, artillery, and international photojournalism exhibitions documenting the war's human cost. It deepens historical context but is emotionally intense; families with small children may prefer cultural alternatives.
